verybody's knows that we met the  americans  we met the japanese  we met the chinese we met the  australians you name it  as organizers wrap up this year's summit  uncertainty over the region's future  remains intended to provide diplomatic  avenues to help solve differences it's  also provided delegates the chance to  wear their grievances for the world to  see jessica washington al jazeera  singapore now earlier we spoke to mia  newens who's from the international  institute for strategic studies she says  a china stance on  taiwan is clear  i think with regards to taiwan in  particular what we saw from general wei  fungha  and state council  was a reiteration of china's stance on  the issue of taiwan and taiwanese  independence i think in some ways that  was a reaction of course to what we  heard from president biden a few weeks  ago in terms of question of whether or  not the united states would intervene  militarily in a taiwan scenario and  whether or not the united states had  moved on from its strategic ambiguity  with regards to its stance on taiwan and  general austin of course said that um  strategic and well signaled that  strategic ambiguity hadn't changed and  of course that there was no support for  taiwan's independence um and that that  policy was still in place um but at the  end of the day i think general way and  china felt the need to re-emphasize  and restate how strong their feelings on  this point are what i found particularly  interesting though was that at the end  of talking about the us's position with  regards to taiwan there was a small  comment with regards to other countries  who might support taiwan as well and  that they will supposedly also not  succeed  from general waste perspective and that  i think pointed to perhaps other  countries in the region or abroad the  concept here is really that the u.s has  no say in the region extra regional  countries as china calls them aren't  here to determine regional security and  that it's up to countries within the  region to determine that of course the  countries within the region also have an  interest in having extra regional  countries and the united states present  and active and engaged in the region as  well so there's a contradiction there
 

